# Reviewed gitleaks findings.
#
# Each line is a FINGERPRINT: <commit>:<file>:<rule>:<line>. That is deliberately
# the narrowest possible suppression - it pins one finding in one historical
# commit. It does NOT disable the rule, so the same pattern appearing anywhere
# else, in any future commit, still fails CI. Never add a bare rule name here.
#
# Nothing goes in this file that was not actually looked at and understood.

# `curl -u "${DEV_LOGIN_USER}:${DEV_LOGIN_PASSWORD}"` in scripts/doctor.sh.
#
# Shell variable interpolation, not a literal credential - the values come from
# .env, which is gitignored. gitleaks matches the SHAPE of the flag and cannot
# tell the two apart, which is the right trade for a secret scanner to make.
#
# It was still worth acting on rather than just silencing: the inline credential
# flag puts the password in argv, readable by any other user on the box via `ps`
# or /proc/<pid>/cmdline. HEAD no longer does this - the credentials are passed
# on stdin via `--config -`. This entry exists only because the branch's own
# history still contains the commit that introduced the line, and rewriting a
# published branch to hide a reviewed false positive is a worse trade than
# recording it here.
